WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.600
Who decides access, and who executes it?

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.350
The book keeps those apart.

3
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.212
Deciding: business and application owners
decide who should have access and why.

4
00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:12.901
The CISO owns the policy
baseline and its exceptions.

5
00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:16.637
And an identity governance office
owns approval chains and reviews.

6
00:00:16.637 --> 00:00:20.425
Executing: identity engineering
builds the policies,

7
00:00:20.425 --> 00:00:23.624
the PIM settings, consent
and access packages.

8
00:00:23.624 --> 00:00:26.563
The help desk handles
assignments under a scoped role.

9
00:00:26.563 --> 00:00:31.250
Security operations handles alerts,
risky sign-ins and response.

10
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:35.837
Assuring: the ISSO owns the evidence
and findings, the authorizing

11
00:00:35.837 --> 00:00:40.925
official owns accepted risk, and
reviewers recertify access on schedule.

12
00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:42.734
The rule that holds it together:

13
00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:46.075
the people who execute access
changes never approve them.

14
00:00:46.075 --> 00:00:50.626
That's why the help desk works under a
custom role that manages assignments only,

15
00:00:50.626 --> 00:00:54.763
while consent and administrator
roles stay with identity engineering.

16
00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:58.569
The old guide gave the help
desk Application Administrator,

17
00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:03.340
which can grant consent and act as any
app, so that rule existed only on paper.
